ABSTRACT='''Small language models can spend much of their output on explicit reasoning without producing correct code. We study whether shortest-correct low-rank adaptation training offers an advantage over simple thinking controls in Qwen3.5-0.8B, 2B, and 4B. The retrospective study combines saved outputs and grades for 464 unique problems: 164 HumanEval+ tasks and 300 LiveCodeBench tasks. Historical summaries average attempts within each problem and then give problems equal weight; completed-control comparisons separately use first attempts. All six LoRA-1 combinations with thinking OFF or a selected thinking limit are complete, with one answer per problem. LoRA plus OFF passes 65, 165, and 300 problems for the three sizes. LoRA plus limits of 512, 1,024, and 2,048 thinking tokens passes 67, 191, and 332 problems, corresponding to 14.4%, 41.2%, and 71.6%. The 2B and 4B trained limits do not establish reliable accuracy gains over the matching free-limit first attempts. The 4B combination generates about 11.4% fewer total output tokens than that reference. OFF without training remains the highest observed complete-set free score for 0.8B. Historical LoRA-2 and loop diagnostics retain their original 234-problem scope. The findings support testing strong free controls before investing in this training procedure. Conclusions remain limited by retrospective selection, historical environment differences, unadjusted intervals, one model family, and grading scope. Token reductions do not directly establish lower latency, cost, or energy use.'''

